// The rest of t_load, split to stay under the per-block action limit.

t_load_p(int png_load_v) {
  print("load spryte-run.png exp 1 = " + png_load_v);
  t_head();
} return void;

// Test-only fixture: main.id's own init order, minus the load itself --
// main/t_load/t_load_p/t_head/t_px/t_sum and t_lace/t_lace_p/t_again (which
// reloads the same file after the interlace refusal) are skipped below,
// tried and measured (2026-09-26): the decoder's per-symbol scratch lists
// used to be allocated fresh on every LZ77 symbol -- idstd's inf_run/
// inf_back/inf_bits, and `id` never frees a list -- which made one decode of
// this file ~990MB/1.2s and put a case over the 1 GiB cap (idc/README.md,
// "Memory ceiling"). Reusing an exported scratch list per inflate call
// (idstd/core/data/seq/byte/inflate) cut that to ~267MB/0.8s, comfortably
// inside the cap. But every case here is a fresh process, and these 8
// functions each call the next, so even one case for any of them reaches
// t_again's second real decode; casing all 8 with two real cases apiece
// measured at ~173s for this project's build, against the 60s project cap
// (idc/tools/casework.sh's rule). Left uncased pending a decision this
// file's own docstring should not make silently: accept the slower build,
// or accept a smaller stand-in for the one asset the header comment says
// has to be the real 2560x2560 sheet. t_one/t_one2/t_one3 (../chk/px/) do
// not need this fixture: they read four bytes at a caller-given address and
// are cased against spr/img/rgba.png instead, real but small.
png_ready() {
  fx_trig_init();
  png_init();
} return void;
():() then calls fx_trig_init:[1]
():() then calls png_init:[1]
